post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

c7shell installed. To put the configs in your home directory, run as your
own user (not root):

    c7shell-setup

Then log out and pick the "c7shell" session in your display manager.

The sddm greeter theme is installed to /usr/share/sddm/themes/c7shell, but
selecting it is a write to /etc that a package must not make. c7shell-bootstrap
does it (see below), or by hand:

    sudo mkdir -p /etc/sddm.conf.d
    printf '[Theme]\nCurrent=c7shell\n' | sudo tee /etc/sddm.conf.d/10-c7shell.conf

If the session does not come up, run:

    c7shell-doctor

It checks everything the session needs that pacman cannot check for you -- the
Hyprland version, quickshell's QML modules, the GPU device, the appmenu
daemon's python modules -- and names the package behind anything missing.
To pick from the optional programs (terminal, file manager, brightness, media
keys, ...) at any time:

    c7shell-doctor --optional

And to (re)configure the system side -- GPU driver, SDDM, pipewire, fonts,
services -- on a machine that was never bootstrapped:

    c7shell-bootstrap --dry-run     # show the plan
    c7shell-bootstrap               # apply it
MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
  cat <<'MSG'

c7shell updated. The files under /usr/share/c7shell changed; your copies
in ~/.config did not. To take the new version:

    c7shell-upgrade --config-only   # keeps your edits, parks new versions as .new

or, to replace your copies outright:

    c7shell-setup --force           # backs up ~/.config/{hypr,quickshell} first

c7shell-upgrade also selects the c7shell greeter theme in sddm if nothing else
has claimed it -- an install from before the theme existed has the files but not
the selection.

MSG
}
